  [38;5;153mclient.py:11,17[39m is the shared client. [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m and [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m both import it and only
  differ by the [38;5;153mREPORTS_BASE_URL[39m they set. So [38;5;153mverify=False[39m on those two calls also disables certificate verification for
  the nightly production export against [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m — that traffic becomes open to interception/MITM
  with no error raised, and it's a silent change nobody sees until it matters.

  Two ways to get staging working without touching production's posture:

  1. [1mPoint [38;5;153mverify[39m at the staging CA cert[0m — keep verification on everywhere, give the self-signed cert a trust anchor.
     [38;5;153mverify[39m accepts a CA bundle path, e.g. driven by a [38;5;153mREPORTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m env var that only [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m sets.
     Production keeps default verification. This is what I'd recommend.
  2. [1mScope the opt-out to staging explicitly[0m — a module-level [38;5;153mVERIFY[39m read from something like [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS[39m,
     defaulting to [38;5;153mTrue[39m, set to false only in [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m. Production stays verified, but the footgun exists and can
     be flipped on prod by an env var.

  Either is a small change. Which do you want — or if you really do want [38;5;153mverify=False[39m hardcoded on both calls in the
  shared client, say so and I'll do exactly that.

